int cold_count = le32_to_cpu(sbi->raw_super->extension_count);
int hot_count = sbi->raw_super->hot_ext_count;
Reason:
The `cold_count` and `hot_count` are read outside `sbi->sb_lock`, while a concurrent `f2fs_update_extension_list()` modifies them under that lock. This race can cause the show function to use a count that is inconsistent with the actual `extension_list` array, potentially resulting in out-of-bounds array access or displaying stale data.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
+		f2fs_down_read(&sbi->sb_lock);
+		cold_count = le32_to_cpu(sbi->raw_super->extension_count);
+		hot_count = sbi->raw_super->hot_ext_count;
 		len += sysfs_emit_at(buf, len, "cold file extension:\n");
 		for (i = 0; i < cold_count; i++)
 			len += sysfs_emit_at(buf, len, "%s\n", extlist[i]);

 		len += sysfs_emit_at(buf, len, "hot file extension:\n");
 		for (i = cold_count; i < cold_count + hot_count; i++)
 			len += sysfs_emit_at(buf, len, "%s\n", extlist[i]);
+		f2fs_up_read(&sbi->sb_lock);
Reason:
Acquiring `sbi->sb_lock` as a reader lock (`f2fs_down_read`) before reading the counts and the extension list ensures that the entire read operation sees a consistent snapshot of the protected data. Releasing the lock after the format completes prevents the race condition, guaranteeing that the counts and the array contents remain in sync.

